{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792228960,"nonce":"kF+nsI9cdp+SwqvgG21rEda7yTM=","title":"String character indexing scans from the start: revcomp ~500x, knucleotide ~90x behind hxcpp","message":"`charCodeAt(i)`, `substr(i, k)`, `charAt`, `indexOf(from)` and `length` map a character index to a byte offset with `char_to_byte(b, i)`, which checks `b[0..i].is_ascii()` on every call (and `length` counts the whole string). A scan over an N-char string is therefore O(N^2).\n\nCI Benchmarks run 38976565897 (2026-10-16, x86-64 runner), warm runtime:\n- revcomp: hxcpp 93.6 ms, rayzor-llvm 46648 ms (x498). Loop is `input.charCodeAt(i)` over a 2.5M-char string.\n- knucleotide: hxcpp 406 ms, rayzor-llvm 36540 ms (x90). Loop is `seq.substr(i, k)` over a 500k-char string, 7 passes.\n\nFix in progress: `HaxeString` gains a `chars` field (character count, lazily counted and cached atomically; `CHARS_UNKNOWN` until then). When `chars == len` every character is one byte, so character and byte offsets coincide and indexing is O(1). Concat and substr carry the count. The duplicate 3-field `HaxeString` in runtime/src/string.rs (which `haxe_string_concat_ptr` boxed and `haxe_string_free` reclaimed as the other layout) is removed; out-pointer writers now write the whole struct.\n\nNon-ASCII strings stay O(i) per index; a breadcrumb (last char index -\u003e byte offset) would cover sequential scans if a benchmark needs it.","files":null}]}